  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Couple rounds of isolated to scattered \nrain/snow showers across the north this weekend, with \naccumulation possible across higher elevations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAn upper low will move south toward the region tonight, then\ncross the forecast area Saturday. The low will bring isolated \nto scattered snow and rain showers to mostly northern and \nnorthwest portions of the forecast area along and northwest of\nthe Longfellow Mtns. The snow showers could possibly produce \nlight accumulations, generally less than an inch, across mostly \nhigher terrain areas.\n\nThe closed upper level low exits to the south Saturday night, \nfollowed by an upper level shortwave passing from south to north\nduring the day on Sunday in its wake. These two systems could \nbring some isolated to scattered snow showers to mainly the \nnorth Saturday night and Sunday (with rain or snow showers \nSunday afternoon across portions of Eastern Aroostook, and \nNorthern Penobscot). While any accumulations should generally be\nat most a few tenths of an inch, any stronger snow showers on \nSunday could produce a quick inch or two across elevations above\n1000 ft especially along . The best chance for this would be \nfor areas along and west of Route 11 north of Ashland and across\nCentral and Northern Piscataquis County.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Persistent dry conditions next week will lead to\npotential worsening of the ongoing water resources drought\nimpacting Eastern and Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nMonday a departing trof will pivot out over the North Atlantic\nand a large 582dam 500mb high pressure dome begins to shift\neast over the Ohio River Valley. This large scale ridging will\ncontrol our weather for all of next week. The northern stream\njet will be pushed north into Central Quebec which will prevent\nany true weather systems to impact the area. This 500mb ridge\nwill feature highly anomalous heights generally 1.5-2.5SD over\nthe Great Lakes but will slowly begin to breakdown as it shifts\nover our area. This will lead to 40s-50s for highs and upper 20s\nto 30s for lows with dry conditions. A few members of the\nECMWF/GFS/Canadian ensembles suggest a possible weak front on\nWed-Thu timeframe but very little reflection for precip.\nAlthough it will be dry there is no significant indication of \nfire weather concerns but given the dry dead vegetation will \nneed to keep an eye on conditions. \n\nOverall these dry conditions will likely lead to some worsening\nof the ongoing water resources drought impacting the CWA. Latest\nUS Drought Monitor has D2-D3 (Severe-Extreme) conditions across\nthe Central Highlands, Northern Maine with the worst areas in\nthe Quebec/Maine borderlands. D1 (Moderate) drought in Coastal\nHancock to Bangor to Lincoln and east to the St. Croix River. D0\n(Abnormally Dry) for Coastal Washington and interior Hancock\ncounties. Surface to 6 feet soil moisture remains heavily\nimpacted from the 2025-2026 drought with soil moisture in the\n2-10th percentile range (Much Below Normal). Surface to 4 \ninches is already once again drying back out and dropping back \ninto the 5-10th percentile (Much Below Normal). Verifying this \nwith USGS Groundwater gages at Clayton Lake and Millinocket \nsitting at Record Low conditions (33-34 years POR). Calais and \nKenduskeag sitting at much below normal and Fort Kent/Hadley \nLakes sitting at below normal conditions. Streamflows that \nrecovered some during the recent rainfall are trending back \ndownward. Of the 38 monitored USGS stream gages in the CWA 22 \nof these gages are at Below to Much Below Normal.",
